 "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/0.jpg", "preference": -9, "id": "28"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/8uBfp06nb64/0.webp", "preference": -8, "id": "29"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEbCKgBEF5IVfKriqkDDggBFQAAiEIYAXABwAEG&rs=AOn4CLAtLZ1qXspuSwvhfUT5BlDBdsbgzw", "height": 94, "width": 168, "preference": -7, "id": "30", "resolution": "168x94"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEbCMQBEG5IVfKriqkDDggBFQAAiEIYAXABwAEG&rs=AOn4CLCDQZ53wkMbU54UyB5_7nXhaU96QQ", "height": 110, "width": 196, "preference": -7, "id": "31", "resolution": "196x110"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEcCPYBEIoBSFXyq4qpAw4IARUAAIhCGAFwAcABBg==&rs=AOn4CLD0x6eLZL9f_RMsOa8cX9u6rHDw1Q", "height": 138, "width": 246, "preference": -7, "id": "32", "resolution": "246x138"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEcCNACELwBSFXyq4qpAw4IARUAAIhCGAFwAcABBg==&rs=AOn4CLD7StKI5cprwSOs8znICN-tRDhgHQ", "height": 188, "width": 336, "preference": -7, "id": "33", "resolution": "336x188"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/hqdefault.jpg", "height": 360, "width": 480, "preference": -7, "id": "34", "resolution": "480x360"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/8uBfp06nb64/hqdefault.webp", "preference": -6, "id": "35"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/sddefault.jpg", "preference": -5, "id": "36"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/8uBfp06nb64/sddefault.webp", "preference": -4, "id": "37"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/hq720.jpg", "preference": -3, "id": "38"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/8uBfp06nb64/hq720.webp", "preference": -2, "id": "39"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/maxresdefault.jpg", "preference": -1, "id": "40"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/8uBfp06nb64/maxresdefault.webp", "preference": 0, "id": "41"}], "thumbnail": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/8uBfp06nb64/hqdefault.jpg", "description": "(13 Sep 2007) SHOTLIST\r\n1. Wide exterior of the International Automobile Federation (FIA) with media waiting outside \r\n2. Mid of FIA flag\r\n3. Mid of media waiting outside\r\n4. Various of President of Formula One Management (FOM), Bernie Ecclestone leaving FIA\r\n5. Members of hearing commission leaving FIA\r\n6. Vice President of Mercedes Benz Motor Sport, Norbert Haug leaving FIA\r\n7. SOUNDBITE: (German) Norbert Haug, Vice President of Mercedes Benz Motor Sport:\r\n\"We will have a news conference so you can all come to that and you will all be told.\"\r\n8. Mercedes Motorsport boss Norbert Haug talking to media whilst leaving\r\n9. McLaren test driver, Pedro Martinez De La Rosa leaving FIA\r\n10. McLaren Team Principal and Chairman and CEO of McLaren Group, Ron Dennis leaving FIA\r\n11. Dennis getting into car whilst being followed by media\r\nSTORYLINE:\r\nThe McLaren team was fined 100 (m) million US dollars and stripped of all its points in the constructors' standings on Thursday, in the spy scandal that has rocked the sport.\r\nTeam drivers Lewis Hamilton and Fernando Alonso, who are currently 1-2 in the championship standings, were not punished and can continue to compete for the season title.\r\nMcLaren could still be penalised for the 2008 championship, the International Automobile Federation (FIA) said in a statement after a hearing.\r\nMcLaren, which leads the current drivers' and constructors' standings, was punished by the World Motor Sports Council for allegedly using leaked secret technical documents belonging to F1 rival Ferrari.\r\nMcLaren can appeal.\r\nThe case broke open in July when a 780-page technical dossier on Ferrari cars was found at the home of McLaren's chief designer, Mike Coughlan, who was later suspended. \r\nFerrari mechanic Nigel Stepney, who allegedly supplied the documents, was fired.\r\nRookie English driver Hamilton leads the standings with 92 points, followed by two-time F1 champion Alonso of Spain with 89.\r\nFerrari team mates Kimi Raikkonen and Felipe Massa  are third and fourth with 74 and 69 points each respectively. \r\nFour races remain in the season, starting with Sunday's Belgian Grand Prix.\r\nHamilton and Alonso finished in first and second place respectively in Sunday's Italian Grand Prix at Ferrari's home track of Monza  to extend McLaren's lead in the constructors' championship to 23 points. \r\nMcLaren had 166, Ferrari 143.\r\nUnder Thursday's ruling, McLaren loses all its constructors' points and is ineligible from scoring any more in the final races of the season.\r\nThe 100 (m) million US dollar penalty includes McLaren's expected loss of income.\r\nMcLaren escaped the harshest possible penalty, as FIA could have kicked the team and its drivers out of the 2007 and 2008 championships. \r\nIn December, FIA will decide on any possible sanctions against McLaren for the 2008 season.\r\nFIA said it did not penalise the McLaren's drivers \"due to exceptional circumstances\" because they provided evidence in exchange for immunity.\r\nAmong those appearing at the hearing before the 26-member council were Hamilton,  team chief Ron Dennis and McLaren test driver Pedro De La Rosa. Alonso did not attend.\r\nAmong others attending were Ross Brawn, Ferrari's former technical director, and team officials from Red Bull, Williams and Spyker.\r\nThe case against McLaren reportedly consists of a 166-page dossier that includes e-mail exchanges between De la Rosa and Alonso as well as details of phone and text message traffic between Coughlan and Stepney supplied to FIA by authorities in Italy.\r\nSeparately, McLaren was notified on Saturday that it is being investigated in a separate criminal inquiry in Italy. \r\n\r\nFind out more about AP Archive: http://www.aparchive.com/HowWeWork \r\nTwitter: https://twitter.com/AP_Archive \r\nFacebook: https://www.facebook.com/APArchives ​​\r\nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/APNews/\r\n\r\n\r\nYou can license this story through AP Archive: http://www.apa